
  <sn><md>### Dose

The science of toxicology is based on the principle that there is a ++relationship++ between a `toxic reaction` (*the response*) and the `amount of poison received` (*the dose*).

&gt; #### "The right dose differentiates a poison from a remedy."
&gt; *Paracelsus*

:::.box bg-red .my-2
**What Is a Dose?**
In toxicology, a dose refers to the `quantity of a substance` that an `organism is exposed` to. 
:::

- But simply saying "10 milligrams of chemical X" doesn't tell us much on its own. A 10 mg exposure will affect a mouse very differently than a human adult. That's why dose is almost always `expressed relative to body weight` – most commonly as `milligrams of substance per kilogram of body weight` (++mg/kg++).
 [see](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/media/toxicology/dose_diff_in_organism.png)

- **Important Assumptions in the Dose-Response Relationship**
	1. There is always a ++dose below++ which `no response occurs` or can be measured.
	2. Once a ++maximum response++ is reached, any `further increases` in the dose will not result in any increased effects.
    [see](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/media/toxicology/dose-response-graph1.png)

- **Dose-Response Connection**
	A basic idea in toxicology and pharmacology, the dose-response connection denotes the `quantitative link between an organism's biological response and the dose of a drug` that is supplied or encountered.Knowing the dose-response connection is crucial for 
	- evaluating risks, 
	- establishing exposure limits, and 
	- directing regulatory choices, regardless of whether one is examining the toxicity of environmental contaminants or the therapeutic efficacy of pharmaceuticals.
